            <div xml:id="d392">

                <pb facs="0641" n="529" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg529"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 10892.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Chargé Garrett</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1908-06-10"><hi rendition="#italic">June 10,
                        1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 842.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt, in due
                    course, of Mr. Tower’s No. 1273, of December 20, 1907, wherein were reported the
                    circumstances <pb facs="0642" n="530" xml:id="pg530"/> attending an altercation
                    between a German tax collector and the porter of the ambassador’s house, a
                    German subject, in consequence of which the ambassador was asked to permit
                    summons to be served on the porter at the house. Mr. Tower discussed the merits
                    of the case and inquired whether he might properly comply with the request.</p>
                <p>In reply I have to say that, if the porter is still in the service of the
                    embassy, the question of his personal immunity may be waived, but that the
                    service should be personal and outside of the embassy precincts.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. Root.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
